1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,179
Start typing a message on your phone, and it

2
00:00:03,179 --> 00:00:06,358
suggests the next word. Now imagine that same idea,

3
00:00:06,358 --> 00:00:09,537
scaled up enormously. That is the core of how

4
00:00:09,537 --> 00:00:12,715
chatbots write whole essays, one word at a time.

5
00:00:12,715 --> 00:00:15,894
By the end of this video, you will understand

6
00:00:15,894 --> 00:00:19,073
how a large language model predicts the next word,

7
00:00:19,073 --> 00:00:21,899
and why that simple trick is so powerful.

8
00:00:22,699 --> 00:00:26,341
Think of your phone's autocomplete. You type, I am

9
00:00:26,341 --> 00:00:29,983
running, and it suggests late. An LLM, short for

10
00:00:29,983 --> 00:00:33,626
large language model, does the same thing at a

11
00:00:33,626 --> 00:00:37,268
much larger scale. It reads everything written so far,

12
00:00:37,268 --> 00:00:39,292
and predicts what comes next.

13
00:00:40,092 --> 00:00:43,465
Here is the key idea. Given the text so

14
00:00:43,465 --> 00:00:46,839
far, the model does not just pick one word.

15
00:00:46,839 --> 00:00:50,213
It gives every possible next word a probability. For

16
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:53,587
the sentence, the cat sat on the, the word

17
00:00:53,587 --> 00:00:56,961
mat might get a high score, sofa a bit

18
00:00:56,961 --> 00:01:00,334
less, floor less again, and a strange word like

19
00:01:00,334 --> 00:01:03,708
moon almost nothing. These numbers are only examples. A

20
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:07,082
real model scores tens of thousands of possible tokens

21
00:01:07,082 --> 00:01:07,832
at once.

22
00:01:08,632 --> 00:01:12,563
Then the model picks one. Usually it chooses a

23
00:01:12,563 --> 00:01:16,494
likely word, but with some randomness, so the same

24
00:01:16,494 --> 00:01:20,425
question can give different answers. A setting called temperature

25
00:01:20,425 --> 00:01:24,356
controls this. Low temperature plays it safe. High temperature

26
00:01:24,356 --> 00:01:25,667
takes more risks.

27
00:01:26,467 --> 00:01:30,046
Now the loop. The model picks a word, adds

28
00:01:30,046 --> 00:01:33,626
it to the text, and predicts again using the

29
00:01:33,626 --> 00:01:37,205
longer text. Predict, add, repeat. That is how a

30
00:01:37,205 --> 00:01:40,784
whole answer is written, one token at a time.

31
00:01:40,784 --> 00:01:43,966
Here it adds mat, then purring, then softly.

32
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:48,124
How does the model learn to do this? By

33
00:01:48,124 --> 00:01:51,483
practice, on a huge amount of text. Take a

34
00:01:51,483 --> 00:01:54,841
sentence and hide the next word. The model guesses.

35
00:01:54,841 --> 00:01:58,199
We check the guess against the real word. Then

36
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:01,558
the model's internal numbers, called weights, are adjusted a

37
00:02:01,558 --> 00:02:04,916
tiny bit, so the right word becomes more likely

38
00:02:04,916 --> 00:02:08,275
next time. Repeat this again and again, an enormous

39
00:02:08,275 --> 00:02:11,633
number of times, and the model gets very good

40
00:02:11,633 --> 00:02:12,379
at predicting.

41
00:02:13,179 --> 00:02:16,469
Why does such a simple task produce something that

42
00:02:16,469 --> 00:02:19,760
seems smart? To predict the next word well across

43
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:23,050
a huge range of text, the model has to

44
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:26,340
pick up grammar, facts, writing styles, and how ideas

45
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:29,631
connect. It also uses a mechanism called attention, to

46
00:02:29,631 --> 00:02:32,921
decide which earlier words matter most for the next

47
00:02:32,921 --> 00:02:36,211
one. Chatbots then get extra training to follow instructions,

48
00:02:36,211 --> 00:02:39,501
but the core is still predicting the next word.

49
00:02:40,301 --> 00:02:43,644
But remember what it is really doing. It predicts

50
00:02:43,644 --> 00:02:46,986
likely text. It does not look facts up by

51
00:02:46,986 --> 00:02:50,328
itself, unless it is given a tool to do

52
00:02:50,328 --> 00:02:53,671
so, which is exactly what AI agents do, as

53
00:02:53,671 --> 00:02:57,013
we saw in our video about agents. So an

54
00:02:57,013 --> 00:03:00,355
LLM can sound confident and still be wrong. Check

55
00:03:00,355 --> 00:03:01,098
anything important.

56
00:03:01,898 --> 00:03:05,849
Let's recap. One: an LLM scores every possible next

57
00:03:05,849 --> 00:03:09,800
word. Two: it picks one, adds it to the

58
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,750
text, and repeats. Three: it learns by practicing on

59
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:17,701
huge amounts of text, adjusting its weights each time.

60
00:03:18,501 --> 00:03:22,100
We mentioned weights being adjusted. In the next video,

61
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:25,698
we look at how a neural network actually learns.

